/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/java-se.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("java-se", [["js1",0,1,"What is the output?\nString s = \"Java\";\ns.concat(\" SE\");\ns = s + 25;\nSystem.out.println(s);",["Java SE25","Java25","Java SE 25","It does not compile"],1,"Strings are immutable and the result of concat() is thrown away, so s is still \"Java\" before 25 is appended. Java SE25 would need s = s.concat(\" SE\").","Handling values: String",["This assumes s.concat(\" SE\") changed s, but String is immutable and the returned string was never assigned.",null,"Besides the discarded concat() result, concat adds no space before 25; string concatenation with + inserts nothing extra.","Every line is legal: concat() can be called and its result ignored, and String + int is ordinary concatenation."],2],["js2",0,1,"What is the output?\nint x = 5;\nint y = x++ + ++x;\nSystem.out.println(x + \" \" + y);",["6 11","7 11","6 12","7 12"],3,"x++ gives 5 and makes x 6; ++x makes x 7 and gives 7, so y = 12 and x = 7. 7 11 comes from treating ++x as 6.","Handling values: operators",["x ends at 7, not 6, because both x++ and ++x increment it; and y is 5 + 7, not 11.","x is 7, but y is 12: ++x increments x from 6 to 7 before yielding 7, so 5 + 7 = 12.","y is 12, but x was incremented twice (once by x++ and once by ++x), so it is 7, not 6.",null],2],["js3",0,1,"What is the output?\nStringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"abc\");\nsb.append(\"de\").reverse().insert(1, \"-\");\nSystem.out.println(sb);",["e-dcba","-edcba","ed-cba","abcde-"],0,"append makes abcde, reverse makes edcba, and insert(1, \"-\") puts the dash after the first character. All three calls change the same StringBuilder, unlike String.","Handling values: StringBuilder",[null,"The dash would be first only with insert(0, \"-\"); insert(1, ...) places it after the first character.","This puts the dash at index 2; insert(1, \"-\") inserts after just one character of \"edcba\".","This ignores reverse(), which changes the StringBuilder in place, and puts the dash at the end instead of index 1."],2],["js4",0,1,"What does this print (brackets included)?\nString t = \"\"\"\n    Hi \\\n    there\"\"\";\nSystem.out.println(\"[\" + t + \"]\");",["[Hi\nthere]","[    Hi there]","[Hi there]","[Hi \\ there]"],2,"The backslash at the end of the line joins the next line, and incidental indentation is stripped, leaving \"Hi there\". It is not a newline, and the shared leading spaces are removed.","Handling values: text blocks",["A trailing backslash is a line-continuation escape, so no newline is inserted between the two lines.","The four leading spaces are incidental indentation shared by all content lines, so the compiler strips them.",null,"The backslash is not kept as a character; at the end of a line it is an escape that joins the lines."],3],["js5",0,1,"What is the output?\nLocalDate d = LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 31);\nSystem.out.println(d.plusMonths(1));",["2024-03-02","2024-02-29","2024-03-01","A DateTimeException is thrown"],1,"plusMonths clamps to the last valid day of the target month, and 2024 is a leap year, so it is Feb 29. It does not roll over into March and does not throw.","Handling values: Date-Time API",["This would be adding 31 days (plusDays(31) lands on 2024-03-02); plusMonths adjusts the day instead of overflowing.",null,"plusMonths never rolls over into the next month; it clamps the day to the last valid day of February.","plusMonths does not throw for a day that does not exist in the target month; it resolves to the month's last valid day."],2],["js6",0,1,"A scheduler stores a ZonedDateTime and must run a job at 09:00 local time every day, including the days when daylight saving time starts or ends. How should it compute the next run?",["next = current.plusDays(1)","next = current.plusHours(24)","next = current.plus(Duration.ofHours(24))","next = current.plusSeconds(86_400)"],0,"plusDays adds a calendar day and keeps the local time of 09:00 across a DST change. Adding 24 hours of exact time (plusHours or a Duration) lands at 08:00 or 10:00 on a transition day.","Handling values: Date-Time API",[null,"plusHours(24) adds exact elapsed time, so on a 23- or 25-hour DST day the result lands at 10:00 or 08:00 local time.","A Duration is exact machine time, so adding 24 hours shifts the local time by an hour across a DST transition.","86,400 seconds is exactly 24 hours of elapsed time, which drifts off 09:00 local time when the day is 23 or 25 hours long."],3],["js7",0,1,"Which line fails to compile?\nbyte b = 10;\nb += 5;      // line 2\nb = b * 2;   // line 3\nshort s = b; // line 4",["Line 2","Line 3","Line 4","All of them compile"],1,"b * 2 is promoted to int, which cannot be assigned to a byte without a cast. Line 2 compiles because compound assignment includes an implicit cast, and line 4 is a widening conversion.","Handling values: casting",["Compound assignment like b += 5 includes an implicit cast back to byte, so line 2 compiles.",null,"Assigning a byte to a short is a widening primitive conversion, which needs no cast.","Line 3 fails: b * 2 is promoted to int and cannot be assigned to a byte without an explicit cast."],2],["js8",0,1,"With default JVM settings, what is the output?\nInteger a = 127, b = 127;\nInteger c = 128, d = 128;\nSystem.out.println((a == b) + \" \" + (c == d));",["true true","false false","false true","true false"],3,"Autoboxing uses Integer.valueOf, which caches -128 to 127, so a and b are the same object while c and d are separate objects. Compare wrapper values with equals().","Handling values: wrapper classes",["128 is outside the default Integer cache of -128 to 127, so c and d are distinct objects and == is false.","127 is inside the Integer cache, so a and b refer to the same cached object and == is true.","This reverses the caching behavior: the small value 127 is cached, and 128 is not.",null],1],["js9",0,1,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(Math.round(-2.5) + \" \" + Math.round(2.5));",["-3 3","-3 2","-2 2","-2 3"],3,"Math.round adds 0.5 and takes the floor, so -2.5 becomes -2 and 2.5 becomes 3. It does not round half away from zero, which would give -3.","Handling values: Math API",["Math.round does not round half away from zero; floor(-2.5 + 0.5) is -2, not -3.","Both halves are wrong: -2.5 rounds up to -2, and 2.5 rounds up to 3, since Math.round is floor(x + 0.5).","2.5 + 0.5 is 3.0, whose floor is 3, so Math.round(2.5) is 3, not 2 (Java does not use banker's rounding here).",null],2],["js10",0,1,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println('a' + 'b' + \"c\");",["abc","195c","ab99","It does not compile"],1,"Evaluation is left to right: 'a' + 'b' is char arithmetic promoted to int (97 + 98 = 195), then string concatenation adds \"c\". abc would need a String operand first.","Handling values: numeric promotion",["abc would need a String on the left; two chars added together are promoted to int before \"c\" is reached.",null,"Neither char becomes a code number followed by a letter; 'a' + 'b' is computed first as 97 + 98.","Adding two chars is legal numeric promotion to int, and int + String is legal concatenation."],1],["js11",0,1,"What is the output?\nString s = \"hello\";\nSystem.out.println(s.substring(1, 3) + s.indexOf('l') + s.charAt(4));",["ell3o","ell2o","el3o","el2o"],3,"substring(1, 3) returns characters 1 and 2 (\"el\"), the first 'l' is at index 2, and charAt(4) is 'o'. The end index of substring is exclusive, so it is not \"ell\".","Handling values: String",["substring(1, 3) excludes index 3, so it gives \"el\", and the first 'l' is at index 2, not 3.","The end index of substring is exclusive, so substring(1, 3) is \"el\", not \"ell\".","\"el\" is right, but indexOf('l') returns the first occurrence, which is index 2, not 3.",null],2],["js12",0,2,"What is the output?\nObject o = 42;\nString r = switch (o) {\n  case Integer i when i > 40 -> \"big\";\n  case Integer i -> \"int\";\n  default -> \"other\";\n};\nSystem.out.println(r);",["int","other","It does not compile","big"],3,"The guarded case is checked first and 42 > 40, so \"big\" is returned. The order is legal because the guarded label comes before the unguarded one.","Program flow: pattern switch",["42 matches the guarded case first because 42 > 40, so the unguarded Integer case is never reached for it.","default is only used when no earlier pattern matches, and 42 is an Integer that matches the first case.","A guarded pattern before an unguarded one of the same type is legal ordering, so there is no dominance error.",null],2],["js13",0,2,"A developer reorders a pattern switch so that `case Integer i -> \"int\";` comes before `case Integer i when i > 40 -> \"big\";`. What happens?",["Compilation fails because the guarded case is dominated","It compiles and \"big\" can never be returned","It compiles, but a warning says the second case is unused","A MatchException is thrown at runtime"],0,"An unguarded type pattern matches every value the later guarded pattern would, so the later label is dominated and the compiler reports an error. It is not just a warning.","Program flow: pattern switch",[null,"The compiler rejects dominated case labels outright; it does not compile a switch with an unreachable case.","Dominance is a compile-time error, not a warning, so the code never compiles.","MatchException is a runtime error for unmatched values in some exhaustive switches; here the code fails at compile time first."],3],["js14",0,2,"What is the output?\nint n = 2;\nswitch (n) {\n  case 1: System.out.print(\"A\");\n  case 2: System.out.print(\"B\");\n  case 3: System.out.print(\"C\"); break;\n  default: System.out.print(\"D\");\n}",["B","BCD","BC","ABC"],2,"Execution starts at case 2 and falls through into case 3 until the break. default is not reached because of that break.","Program flow: switch statement",["Without a break after case 2, execution falls through into case 3 and also prints C.","The break at the end of case 3 stops execution, so default is never reached.",null,"Execution jumps straight to the matching label case 2; case 1's code before it is not run."],1],["js15",0,2,"What is the output?\nouter:\nfor (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n  for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {\n    if (j == 2) continue outer;\n    if (i == 2) break outer;\n    System.out.print(i + \"\" + j + \" \");\n  }\n}",["00 01 10 11 20 21 ","00 01 10 11 ","00 01 02 10 11 12 ","00 01 "],1,"For i = 0 and i = 1, j prints 0 and 1 and then continue outer skips j = 2. When i = 2, break outer ends both loops before anything prints.","Program flow: labeled break and continue",["When i is 2, break outer ends both loops before anything prints, so no 2x pairs appear.",null,"continue outer runs when j == 2, before the print, so pairs ending in 2 never print.","continue outer only skips to the next i; it does not end the outer loop, so the i = 1 pairs are printed too."],3],["js16",0,2,"An enum Level has constants LOW, MEDIUM and HIGH. A switch expression over a Level variable has cases for all three and no default. What is true?",["It needs a default label or it will not compile","It compiles; if a new constant is added later, the switch no longer compiles until updated","It compiles only when written as a statement, not an expression","It compiles, and new constants fall through to the last case"],1,"A switch expression over an enum is exhaustive when every constant is covered, so no default is required. Adding a constant makes it non-exhaustive and the compiler flags it.","Program flow: switch expressions",["A switch expression over an enum is exhaustive when all constants are covered, so no default is required.",null,"Switch expressions support enums without a default when every constant has a case; there is no such restriction.","Arrow-form switch expressions never fall through; an uncovered new constant makes the switch non-exhaustive and it no longer compiles."],2],["js17",0,2,"What is the output?\nint i = 10;\ndo {\n  i++;\n} while (i < 5);\nSystem.out.println(i);",["10","11","5","The loop never ends"],1,"A do-while body always runs at least once, so i becomes 11 before the condition is checked and found false. A while loop would leave i at 10.","Program flow: loops",["A do-while runs its body before checking the condition, so i is incremented to 11 at least once.",null,"Nothing sets i to 5; the condition i < 5 is only a test and it is false after the first pass.","After one pass i is 11 and i < 5 is false, so the loop ends immediately."],1],["js18",0,2,"What is the output?\nint day = 3;\nString s = switch (day) {\n  case 1, 7 -> \"weekend\";\n  default -> {\n    String t = \"weekday\";\n    yield t.toUpperCase();\n  }\n};\nSystem.out.println(s);",["weekday","It does not compile because of yield","weekend","WEEKDAY"],3,"3 matches default, whose block returns a value with yield. yield is how a block in a switch expression returns a value; return is not allowed there.","Program flow: switch expressions",["The block calls toUpperCase() before yielding, so the result is uppercase.","yield is exactly how a block body in a switch expression returns a value, so it compiles.","3 is not listed in case 1, 7, so the default block handles it.",null],1],["js19",0,3,"Given `record Point(int x, int y) {}`, which statement is true?",["It has a no-argument constructor and setters for x and y","equals() compares object identity unless you override it","It can extend another class that defines x and y","Its fields are private and final, and p.x() returns the x component"],3,"Records get private final fields, a canonical constructor and accessors named after the components, plus equals, hashCode and toString based on the components. Records are implicitly final and cannot extend classes.","OO: records",["Records have no setters and only a canonical constructor by default; a no-arg constructor exists only if the record has no components or you declare one that delegates.","A record's generated equals() compares component values, not object identity.","Records implicitly extend java.lang.Record and are final, so they cannot extend another class.",null],1],["js20",0,3,"What is the output?\nclass A { String name = \"A\"; String get() { return name; } }\nclass B extends A { String name = \"B\"; String get() { return name; } }\n// in main:\nA a = new B();\nSystem.out.println(a.name + a.get());",["AA","BB","BA","AB"],3,"Fields are not polymorphic: a.name uses the reference type A. get() is overridden, so the call runs B's version, which returns B's field.","OO: polymorphism",["get() is overridden and dispatched on the runtime type B, so it returns B's field value \"B\", not \"A\".","a.name is resolved from the reference type A, because fields are hidden, not overridden, so it gives \"A\".","This swaps the rules: the field access uses the compile-time type (A) and the method call uses the runtime type (B).",null],3],["js21",0,3,"What is the output?\nclass P { static String id() { return \"P\"; } }\nclass C extends P { static String id() { return \"C\"; } }\n// in main:\nP p = new C();\nSystem.out.println(p.id());",["P","C","It does not compile","PC"],0,"Static methods are hidden, not overridden, so the call is chosen from the reference's compile-time type P. Calling a static method through a reference compiles, with at most a warning.","OO: method hiding",[null,"Static methods are hidden, not overridden, so the runtime type C does not affect which id() runs.","Calling a static method through an instance reference is legal Java; at most it produces a compiler warning.","Only one method is called; hiding does not chain the parent and child versions."],2],["js22",0,3,"On Java 25, what is the output of `new Sub(3);`?\nclass Base { Base(int v) { System.out.print(\"Base\" + v + \" \"); } }\nclass Sub extends Base {\n  Sub(int v) {\n    if (v < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n    System.out.print(\"check \");\n    super(v * 2);\n    System.out.print(\"Sub\");\n  }\n}",["Base6 check Sub","It does not compile: super() must be the first statement","check Base6 Sub","check Base3 Sub"],2,"Java 25 flexible constructor bodies allow statements before super(...) if they do not use the object under construction. The prologue runs first, then Base with 6, then the rest of the body.","OO: flexible constructor bodies",["Statements before super(...) in the prologue run first, so \"check \" prints before the Base constructor.","Java 25 flexible constructor bodies (JEP 513) allow statements before super(...) as long as they don't use this.",null,"The argument passed is v * 2, so Base receives 6, not 3."],2],["js23",0,3,"In a Java 25 subclass constructor, which statement is NOT allowed before the explicit super(...) call?",["A local variable declaration that computes the argument","An if statement that throws for a bad argument","A call to an instance method, such as this.validate(v)","Assigning a parameter to a field that has no initializer"],2,"Code before super(...) cannot use the instance being built, so calling an instance method is rejected. Local variables, argument checks and assigning fields without initializers are allowed.","OO: flexible constructor bodies",["Declaring local variables to compute the super argument is a main use of the constructor prologue and is allowed.","Validating arguments and throwing before super(...) is allowed because it does not touch the object being built.",null,"Java 25 specifically allows assigning fields that have no initializer before super(...), so this is permitted."],3],["js24",0,3,"Why does this fail to compile?\nsealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square {}\nrecord Circle(double r) implements Shape {}\nclass Square implements Shape {}",["A record cannot implement a sealed interface","Square must be declared final, sealed or non-sealed","The permits list must use fully qualified names","A sealed interface cannot have more than one implementation"],1,"Every permitted subclass must say how it continues the hierarchy: final, sealed or non-sealed. Records are implicitly final, so Circle is fine.","OO: sealed types",["Records can implement interfaces, including sealed ones, and are implicitly final, which satisfies the sealed rules.",null,"Simple names are fine in a permits clause when the types are in scope (same file or package).","A sealed interface can permit any number of subtypes listed in its permits clause."],2],["js25",0,3,"A method uses `return switch (shape) { case Circle(double r) -> ...; case Square(double s) -> ...; };` over a sealed interface. A developer adds a Triangle record to the permits list but doesn't change the method. What happens?",["The method stops compiling because the switch is no longer exhaustive","It compiles, and Triangle shapes return 0.0","It compiles, but Triangle shapes throw a NullPointerException","The permits change is rejected until a default is added elsewhere"],0,"The compiler checks exhaustiveness over the sealed hierarchy, so a missing permitted subtype is a compile error. That is the benefit of pattern switches over sealed types with no default.","OO: sealed types and record patterns",[null,"Java does not supply a default value for an unmatched case; a pattern switch with no default must be exhaustive or fail to compile.","No runtime fallback happens because the non-exhaustive switch is caught by the compiler first.","Changing the permits list is legal on its own; the error appears in the switch that no longer covers every subtype."],2],["js26",0,3,"What is the output of `System.out.println(new Z().hi());`?\ninterface X { default String hi() { return \"X\"; } }\ninterface Y { default String hi() { return \"Y\"; } }\nclass Z implements X, Y {\n  public String hi() { return X.super.hi() + Y.super.hi(); }\n}",["XY","X","It does not compile","YX"],0,"Z must override hi() because the defaults conflict, and it calls each interface's version with InterfaceName.super.hi(). Without the override, the class would not compile.","OO: interfaces",[null,"The override calls both X.super.hi() and Y.super.hi() and concatenates them, so it is not just X.","Z resolves the default-method conflict by overriding hi(), and Interface.super.method() is valid syntax for this.","The concatenation order is X first, then Y, so the result is XY."],1],["js27",0,3,"What happens when this method is called with \"text\"?\nstatic void show(Object o) {\n  if (!(o instanceof String s)) return;\n  System.out.println(s.length());\n}",["It prints 4","It does not compile because s is out of scope","It prints 0","It throws a ClassCastException"],0,"Flow scoping puts s in scope after the if, because the method returns whenever the pattern does not match. So s is definitely a String on the println line.","OO: pattern matching for instanceof",[null,"Flow scoping puts s in scope after the if, because the method returns whenever o is not a String.","s is \"text\", whose length is 4; nothing resets it to empty.","instanceof checks the type before binding, so no unchecked cast happens and no ClassCastException can occur."],2],["js28",0,3,"What is the output?\nenum Size {\n  S(1), M(2), L(3);\n  final int v;\n  Size(int v) { this.v = v; }\n}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(Size.valueOf(\"M\").ordinal() + Size.L.v);",["5","13","4","3"],2,"ordinal() is zero-based, so M is 1, and L.v is 3, giving 4 with int addition. 13 would need string concatenation, and 5 would use M's field value.","OO: enums",["This uses M's field value 2 instead of its ordinal 1; ordinal() is the zero-based position.","13 would require string concatenation, but both operands are ints, so + is numeric addition.",null,"This would be ordinal 0 plus 3; M is the second constant, so its ordinal is 1."],2],["js29",0,3,"What does `new Init(); new Init();` print?\nclass Init {\n  static { System.out.print(\"S\"); }\n  { System.out.print(\"I\"); }\n  Init() { System.out.print(\"C\"); }\n}",["SICSIC","SICIC","ICSIC","SCISCI"],1,"The static block runs once when the class is first used. Each new object then runs instance initializers before the constructor body.","OO: initialization order",["The static initializer runs only once, when the class is first initialized, not per object.",null,"The static block runs before the first instance initializer, so S comes first.","Instance initializers run before the constructor body, so I prints before C, and S appears only once."],2],["js30",0,3,"What is printed by `m(5);`?\nstatic void m(long x)    { System.out.println(\"long\"); }\nstatic void m(Integer x) { System.out.println(\"Integer\"); }\nstatic void m(Object x)  { System.out.println(\"Object\"); }",["Integer","Object","It does not compile: ambiguous","long"],3,"Overload resolution tries widening before boxing, so int widens to long. Integer and Object need boxing and are only used if no primitive match works.","OO: overloading",["Boxing to Integer is only considered when no widening match exists, and int widens to long.","Object also needs boxing first, and widening to long is chosen before any boxing.","The phases of overload resolution pick a single most-specific method, widening to long, so there is no ambiguity.",null],3],["js31",0,3,"A team must make a Money class immutable. It has a String currency and a List<String> history field. Which design is needed?",["Make the fields public final so no one can reassign them","Declare setters private and keep the default constructor","Make the class final, fields private final, copy the list in and out","Mark the class final and the list field volatile"],2,"Immutability needs no subclassing, fields that cannot be reassigned, no setters and defensive copies of mutable parts like the List. A public final list can still be changed through its methods.","OO: encapsulation and immutability",["public final stops reassignment but not mutation; callers can still change the List's contents.","Having private setters and a default constructor doesn't stop mutation of the list and leaves fields assignable inside the class.",null,"volatile controls cross-thread visibility, not mutability; the list can still be modified."],3],["js32",0,3,"`Inner` is a non-static inner class of `Outer`. Which statement creates an Inner object from a static method of another class?",["Outer.Inner i = new Outer.Inner();","Outer.Inner i = Outer.new Inner();","Outer.Inner i = new Inner(new Outer());","Outer.Inner i = new Outer().new Inner();"],3,"An inner (non-static) class instance needs an enclosing instance, written outerRef.new Inner(). new Outer.Inner() only works for a static nested class.","OO: nested classes",["new Outer.Inner() only works for a static nested class; an inner class needs an enclosing instance.","The outer must be an instance, not the class name; Outer.new Inner() is not valid syntax.","An inner class is not created by passing the outer object as a constructor argument; that syntax does not compile.",null],1],["js33",0,3,"After these lines run, how many of the two created objects are eligible for garbage collection?\nObject a = new Object(); // obj1\nObject b = new Object(); // obj2\na = b;\nb = null;",["0","1","2","It cannot be known until System.gc() runs"],1,"obj1 has no references once a points to obj2. obj2 is still referenced by a, so only one object is eligible. Eligibility does not depend on whether the collector has run.","OO: garbage collection",["obj1 lost its only reference when a was reassigned to obj2, so one object is eligible.",null,"obj2 is still referenced by a, so it is not eligible; setting b to null did not remove all references.","Eligibility depends only on reachability, not on whether the garbage collector has run."],2],["js34",0,3,"Which statement about abstract classes is true?",["They cannot declare constructors","All of their methods must be abstract","A class can extend two abstract classes if their methods differ","They can have constructors and state but cannot be instantiated"],3,"An abstract class can have fields, constructors (called by subclasses) and concrete methods, but `new` cannot be used on it. Java has single class inheritance.","OO: abstract classes",["Abstract classes can declare constructors, which subclass constructors call through super(...).","An abstract class can mix abstract and concrete methods, and can even have no abstract methods.","Java allows only single class inheritance, abstract or not; a class extends at most one class.",null],1],["js35",0,3,"What is the output?\nrecord Pair(String a, int b) {}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(new Pair(\"x\", 1));",["Pair[a=x, b=1]","Pair(a=x, b=1)","Pair@1b6d3586","{a=x, b=1}"],0,"A record's generated toString uses the form Name[component=value, ...]. The hash-code form comes from Object.toString, which records override.","OO: records",[null,"Records use square brackets in toString, not parentheses (the parenthesized form is a Lombok style).","Records override toString, so the Object-style class@hash form is never used.","Curly-brace key=value output is Map.toString; a record's toString starts with its name and uses brackets."],1],["js36",0,3,"A method must be visible to classes in the same package and to subclasses in other packages, but not to unrelated classes elsewhere. Which modifier should be used?",["private","no modifier (package access)","protected","public"],2,"protected gives package access plus access from subclasses. Package access alone leaves out subclasses in other packages.","OO: access modifiers",["private limits access to the declaring class, hiding it from the package and subclasses.","Package access excludes subclasses in other packages, which this requirement needs.",null,"public would also expose the method to unrelated classes in other packages, which is not wanted."],1],["js37",0,3,"Which local variable declaration compiles?",["var n = null;","var nums = {1, 2, 3};","var x;","var list = new ArrayList<String>();"],3,"var needs an initializer with a type the compiler can infer. null has no type, var cannot be declared without a value, and an array initializer needs an explicit type.","OO: var",["null has no type the compiler can infer, so var n = null is rejected.","An array initializer needs an explicit array type such as int[]; var cannot infer it from braces alone.","var requires an initializer in the same statement to infer the type, so a bare declaration fails.",null],1],["js38",0,3,"Two default methods in an interface share 15 lines of validation logic. The developer wants to share that code without exposing it to implementing classes. What should they use?",["A private method in the interface","A protected method in the interface","A public static method in the interface","A default method marked final"],0,"Interfaces can have private methods (since Java 9) that default methods call but implementers cannot see. Interface methods cannot be protected or final.","OO: interfaces",[null,"Interface methods cannot be protected; they can only be public or private.","A public static method is visible to every class, so it does not hide the helper from implementers.","Interface methods cannot be final, and a default method would still be visible and overridable."],2],["js39",0,4,"What is the output of `System.out.println(f());`?\nstatic int f() {\n  try { return 1; }\n  finally { System.out.print(\"F\"); }\n}",["1F","1","It does not compile","F1"],3,"The return value 1 is saved, the finally block prints F, and then the method returns and println prints 1. finally always runs before the method actually returns.","Exceptions: try/finally",["println cannot print 1 until f() returns, and finally runs before the return completes, so F comes first.","finally always runs, so F is printed as well.","A try with only a finally block and a return is legal Java.",null],2],["js40",0,4,"Why does this fail to compile?\ntry { new FileReader(\"data.txt\"); }\ncatch (FileNotFoundException | IOException e) { }",["FileReader does not throw checked exceptions","Multi-catch types must not be related by subclassing","A multi-catch needs a finally block","The variable e must be declared final"],1,"FileNotFoundException is a subclass of IOException, and multi-catch types cannot be related by subclassing. The catch variable is already implicitly final.","Exceptions: multi-catch",["The FileReader(String) constructor declares the checked FileNotFoundException.",null,"A multi-catch does not need a finally block; finally is always optional when a catch exists.","A multi-catch parameter is already implicitly final, so no explicit final is needed."],2],["js41",0,4,"R prints \"close\" + its name when closed. What is the output?\ntry (R a = new R(\"A\"); R b = new R(\"B\")) {\n  System.out.print(\"body \");\n} finally {\n  System.out.print(\"fin\");\n}",["body closeA closeB fin","body fin closeB closeA","closeB closeA body fin","body closeB closeA fin"],3,"Resources close in reverse order of declaration, right after the body and before finally. finally never runs before the resources are closed.","Exceptions: try-with-resources",["Resources are closed in reverse order of declaration, so B closes before A.","Resources are closed right after the body, before finally runs, not after it.","Resources are closed after the body runs, not before it.",null],2],["js42",0,4,"In a try-with-resources block, the body throws IllegalStateException and then the resource's close() throws IOException. What does the caller receive?",["The IOException, with the IllegalStateException as its cause","Both exceptions, one after the other","The IllegalStateException, with the IOException as a suppressed exception","Only the IOException; the first exception is lost"],2,"The body's exception is primary, and the one from close() is attached to it with addSuppressed. It can be read with getSuppressed(). This is an improvement on hand-written finally blocks, which lose the first exception.","Exceptions: suppressed exceptions",["The exception from the body stays primary; the close() exception is not wrapped around it as a cause.","Java delivers one exception to the caller; the second is attached to the first rather than thrown separately.",null,"try-with-resources keeps the first exception and adds the close() failure as suppressed, so nothing is lost."],3],["js43",0,4,"A superclass method is declared `void load() throws IOException`. Which subclass override compiles?",["void load() throws Exception","void load() throws Throwable","void load() throws IOException, SQLException","void load() throws FileNotFoundException"],3,"An override can throw the same, narrower or no checked exceptions. FileNotFoundException is narrower than IOException; Exception, Throwable and SQLException are broader or new checked types.","Exceptions: overriding and throws",["Exception is broader than IOException, and an override cannot throw broader checked exceptions.","Throwable is broader than IOException, so this override would widen the checked exceptions.","SQLException is a new checked exception not covered by IOException, which overrides may not add.",null],1],["js44",0,4,"Why does this fail to compile?\ntry { reader.read(); }\ncatch (Exception e) { }\ncatch (IOException e) { }",["read() does not throw IOException","IOException is already caught by the earlier catch block","A try block can only have one catch","Exception is unchecked and cannot be caught"],1,"The catch for Exception already covers IOException, so the second block can never be reached and the compiler rejects it. More specific catch blocks must come first.","Exceptions: catch order",["read() on a Reader does declare IOException, so that is not the problem.",null,"A try block can have many catch blocks; the problem is their order.","Exception is a checked exception class and can certainly be caught."],1],["js45",0,4,"A library needs an InsufficientFundsException that callers must handle or declare, so the compiler enforces it. How should it be declared?",["class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception","class InsufficientFundsException extends RuntimeException","class InsufficientFundsException extends Error","class InsufficientFundsException implements Throwable"],0,"Subclasses of Exception that are not RuntimeException are checked, so the compiler makes callers handle them. RuntimeException subclasses are unchecked, and Throwable is a class, not an interface.","Exceptions: custom exceptions",[null,"Subclasses of RuntimeException are unchecked, so the compiler would not force callers to handle it.","Error is for serious JVM problems that applications are not expected to handle, and it is unchecked.","Throwable is a class, not an interface, so it cannot be implemented."],1],["js46",0,5,"What happens at runtime?\nList<Integer> nums = List.of(1, 2, 3);\nnums.set(0, 9);",["The list becomes [9, 2, 3]","An UnsupportedOperationException is thrown","An IndexOutOfBoundsException is thrown","It does not compile"],1,"List.of returns an unmodifiable list, so every change method throws UnsupportedOperationException. It compiles because set is part of the List interface.","Collections: unmodifiable lists",["List.of returns an unmodifiable list, so set cannot change it.",null,"Index 0 exists in a three-element list, so the index is not the problem; the list rejects all changes.","set is declared in the List interface, so it compiles; the failure happens only at runtime."],1],["js47",0,5,"What is the output?\nString[] arr = {\"a\", \"b\"};\nList<String> list = Arrays.asList(arr);\nlist.set(0, \"z\");\nSystem.out.println(arr[0]);",["a","null","An UnsupportedOperationException is thrown","z"],3,"Arrays.asList returns a fixed-size list backed by the array, so set writes through to the array. Only add and remove, which change the size, throw.","Collections: Arrays.asList",["The list from Arrays.asList is backed by the array, so set(0, \"z\") writes through to arr[0].","Nothing sets the element to null; the list write replaces \"a\" with \"z\" in the array.","Arrays.asList supports set because it does not change the size; only add and remove throw.",null],2],["js48",0,5,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(new TreeSet<>(List.of(\"banana\", \"Apple\", \"cherry\")));",["[banana, Apple, cherry]","[cherry, banana, Apple]","[banana, cherry, Apple]","[Apple, banana, cherry]"],3,"TreeSet uses natural String order, where uppercase letters come before lowercase ones. Insertion order is not kept.","Collections: TreeSet",["TreeSet sorts its elements and does not keep insertion order.","This is reverse order; TreeSet uses ascending natural order unless given another Comparator.","Natural String order compares character codes, so uppercase 'A' sorts before lowercase letters.",null],2],["js49",0,5,"What is the output?\nMap<String, Integer> m = new HashMap<>();\nfor (String w : \"a b a c a\".split(\" \"))\n  m.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum);\nSystem.out.println(m.get(\"a\"));",["1","5","null","3"],3,"merge puts 1 for a new key and otherwise combines the old value with 1 using Integer::sum, so \"a\" ends up with 3. It does not replace the old value with 1.","Collections: Map.merge",["merge adds to the existing value with Integer::sum rather than replacing it with 1.","5 is the number of words in total; only three of them are \"a\".","\"a\" is in the map, so get returns its count, not null.",null],1],["js50",0,5,"What is the output?\nDeque<Integer> d = new ArrayDeque<>();\nd.push(1);\nd.push(2);\nd.offer(3);\nSystem.out.println(d.pop() + \" \" + d.peekLast());",["3 1","1 3","2 1","2 3"],3,"push adds to the front ([2, 1]) and offer adds to the back ([2, 1, 3]). pop removes the front element 2, and peekLast shows 3.","Collections: Deque",["pop removes from the front, where push put 2, not the 3 that offer added at the back.","The front element is 2 (the last push) and peekLast is 3, so 1 3 has both ends wrong.","peekLast looks at the tail, where offer(3) added 3, not 1.",null],3],["js51",0,5,"What is the output?\nList<Integer> l = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20, 30));\nl.remove(1);\nl.remove(Integer.valueOf(10));\nSystem.out.println(l);",["[30]","[20, 30]","[10, 30]","[]"],0,"remove(1) uses the int index overload and removes 20; remove(Integer.valueOf(10)) removes by value. [20, 30] would come from reading remove(1) as removing the value 1.","Collections: List.remove overloads",[null,"remove(1) with an int argument uses the index overload, removing 20, not the value 1.","remove(1) removes the element at index 1 (20), and the second call removes 10, so 10 does not remain.","Only two of the three elements are removed, so 30 remains."],3],["js52",0,5,"A list of words must be sorted by length, and words of the same length alphabetically. Which Comparator does this?",["Comparator.comparing(String::length).reversed()","Comparator.naturalOrder().thenComparing(String::length)","Comparator.comparing(String::length).thenComparing(Comparator.naturalOrder())","Comparator.comparing(String::toString).thenComparing(String::length)"],2,"The first key is length and ties are broken by natural String order. Starting with natural order sorts alphabetically first, so length would almost never matter.","Collections: Comparator",["reversed() sorts by length descending and adds no alphabetical tie-break.","Natural order sorts alphabetically first, so length would only break ties between equal strings.",null,"Sorting by toString is alphabetical order first, so length barely matters."],2],["js53",0,5,"Given `void add(List<? extends Number> nums)`, which statement inside the method compiles?",["Number n = nums.get(0);","nums.add(Double.valueOf(2.0));","nums.add(1);","nums.add(new Object());"],0,"With ? extends Number you can read elements as Number, but you cannot add (except null) because the real element type might be List<Double> or List<Integer>. Use ? super for adding.","Collections: generics and wildcards",[null,"With ? extends Number the list may really be a List<Integer>, so adding a Double is not type-safe and is rejected.","The list may be a List<Double>, so even an Integer cannot be added; only null can be added.","Object is not a Number, and nothing except null can be added to a ? extends list."],3],["js54",0,5,"What is the output (Java 21 or later)?\nList<String> l = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"));\nl.addFirst(\"z\");\nSystem.out.println(l.reversed().getFirst() + l.getFirst());",["zc","ca","cz","az"],2,"addFirst makes [z, a, b, c]. reversed() is a reversed view whose first element is c, and the list's own first element is z.","Collections: sequenced collections",["reversed().getFirst() is c and getFirst() is z, so the order printed is c then z.","addFirst(\"z\") put z at the front, so the list's first element is z, not a.",null,"The reversed view's first element is the last element c, not a."],2],["js55",0,6,"What is the output?\nOptional<Integer> r = Stream.of(1, 2, 3, 4)\n  .peek(n -> System.out.print(n))\n  .filter(n -> n > 1)\n  .findFirst();\nSystem.out.println(\" \" + r.get());",["1234 2","12 2","1 2","2 2"],1,"Streams process elements one at a time and findFirst short-circuits, so only 1 and 2 go through peek. The whole list is not consumed first.","Streams: lazy evaluation",["Streams process elements lazily one at a time, and findFirst stops once 2 passes the filter, so 3 and 4 never reach peek.",null,"1 fails the filter, so the stream moves on and peeks 2 before findFirst can succeed.","1 is peeked before the filter rejects it, so 1 is printed too."],3],["js56",0,6,"What is the output?\nStream.of(\"a\", \"b\")\n  .peek(System.out::print)\n  .map(String::toUpperCase);\nSystem.out.println(\"done\");",["abdone","ABdone","done","It does not compile"],2,"The pipeline has no terminal operation, so nothing runs and peek never prints. Only intermediate operations were set up.","Streams: lazy evaluation",["No terminal operation is called, so peek never runs and a and b are not printed.","Nothing runs without a terminal operation, and even if it did, peek sits before map and would print lowercase a and b.",null,"An unused stream pipeline compiles; nothing forces a terminal operation."],2],["js57",0,6,"What does m.get(2) return?\nMap<Integer, Long> m = Stream.of(\"aa\", \"b\", \"cc\", \"ddd\")\n  .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(String::length, Collectors.counting()));",["1","[aa, cc]","4","2"],3,"Grouping by length with counting() gives {1=1, 2=2, 3=1}, so two strings have length 2. [aa, cc] would be the result without a downstream collector (toList by default).","Streams: groupingBy",["1 is the count for lengths 1 and 3; two strings (aa and cc) have length 2.","The list of strings is what groupingBy gives without a downstream collector; counting() gives a Long count instead.","4 is the total number of strings, not the number with length 2.",null],1],["js58",0,6,"Stream.of(1, 3).collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(n -> n > 5)) is evaluated. What is the result?",["{false=[1, 3]}","{true=[], false=[1, 3]} (keys in some order)","An empty map","{false=[1, 3], true=null}"],1,"partitioningBy always returns a map with both true and false keys, and an empty list for a partition with no elements. groupingBy would have only the false key.","Streams: partitioningBy",["partitioningBy always contains both the true and false keys, even when a partition is empty.",null,"The map is never empty; it always has true and false keys.","An empty partition maps to an empty list, not null."],2],["js59",0,6,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(Stream.of(1, 2, 3, 4).reduce(10, Integer::sum));",["10","20","Optional[20]","Optional[10]"],1,"The identity 10 is the starting value, and 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 is added to it. The overload with an identity returns T, not Optional.","Streams: reduce",["This ignores the elements; the identity is only the starting value, and 1 through 4 are added to it.",null,"reduce with an identity argument returns T directly, not an Optional.","This is both wrapped in Optional and ignores the elements, and the identity overload returns a plain value."],1],["js60",0,6,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 4).map(i -> i * i).sum());",["14","30","10","55"],1,"rangeClosed includes 4, so the squares are 1 + 4 + 9 + 16 = 30. range(1, 4) would stop at 3 and give 14.","Streams: primitive streams",["14 is the sum of squares from 1 to 3, which range(1, 4) would give; rangeClosed includes 4.",null,"10 is 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 without squaring; map squares each value first.","55 is the sum of squares up to 5, but the range stops at 4."],1],["js61",0,6,"A developer stores a stream in a variable, calls count() on it, then calls count() again on the same variable. What happens?",["The second call throws IllegalStateException","The second call returns the same count","The second call returns 0","It does not compile"],0,"A stream can only be used once; a second terminal operation throws IllegalStateException. The compiler cannot detect this.","Streams: stream lifecycle",[null,"A stream cannot be reused after a terminal operation, so it cannot return the count again.","The stream does not reset to empty; using it again throws an exception.","The compiler does not track stream use, so the code compiles and fails only at runtime."],1],["js62",0,6,"Which functional interface type can be the target of the lambda `(a, b) -> a + b` that takes two Integers and returns an Integer?",["BinaryOperator<Integer>","Function<Integer, Integer>","Supplier<Integer>","UnaryOperator<Integer>"],0,"BinaryOperator<T> takes two T values and returns a T. Function and UnaryOperator take a single argument, and Supplier takes none.","Streams: functional interfaces",[null,"Function<Integer, Integer> takes one argument, but the lambda has two parameters.","Supplier takes no arguments, so a two-parameter lambda cannot target it.","UnaryOperator takes one argument, which does not match a two-parameter lambda."],1],["js63",0,6,"Why does this fail to compile?\nint count = 0;\nList.of(1, 2, 3).forEach(x -> count++);",["forEach does not accept a lambda","A local variable used in a lambda must be final or effectively final","count must be declared as an Integer","x is never used"],1,"Lambdas can only use local variables that are effectively final, and count++ changes it. Use an AtomicInteger or a stream count() instead.","Streams: lambdas and effectively final",["forEach takes a Consumer, and a lambda is a valid Consumer.",null,"Changing to Integer would not help; count++ still reassigns a captured local variable.","An unused lambda parameter is legal Java and causes no compile error."],1],["js64",0,6,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(List.of(List.of(1, 2), List.of(3)).stream()\n  .flatMap(List::stream)\n  .map(i -> i * 2)\n  .toList());",["[[2, 4], [6]]","It does not compile","[1, 2, 3]","[2, 4, 6]"],3,"flatMap turns the nested lists into one stream of 1, 2, 3, which are then doubled. map alone would keep the nested structure.","Streams: flatMap",["This nested result would come from map, but flatMap flattens the inner lists into one stream.","flatMap(List::stream) is a valid method reference returning a Stream, so it compiles.","map(i -> i * 2) doubles each element, so the values are not left as 1, 2, 3.",null],1],["js65",0,6,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(Stream.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\")\n  .collect(Collectors.joining(\",\", \"[\", \"]\")));",["[a,b,c]","a,b,c","[a],[b],[c]","[a, b, c]"],0,"joining(delimiter, prefix, suffix) puts the prefix and suffix around the whole result, and the delimiter has no space. [a, b, c] is how List.toString looks.","Streams: Collectors.joining",[null,"The prefix [ and suffix ] are included because the three-argument joining was used.","The prefix and suffix wrap the whole result once, not each element.","The delimiter is exactly \",\" with no space; [a, b, c] is how List.toString formats."],1],["js66",0,6,"On Java 25, what is the output?\nSystem.out.println(Stream.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\n  .gather(Gatherers.windowFixed(2))\n  .toList());",["[[1, 2], [3, 4]]","[[1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4], [4, 5]]","[[1, 2], [3, 4], [5]]","[3, 7, 5]"],2,"windowFixed groups elements into lists of the given size, and the last window can be shorter. Overlapping windows come from windowSliding.","Streams: Stream Gatherers",["windowFixed keeps the final shorter window, so 5 is not dropped.","Overlapping windows are produced by windowSliding, not windowFixed.",null,"windowFixed produces lists, not sums; summing would need another step such as map."],1],["js67",0,6,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(Optional.ofNullable(null).map(Object::toString).orElse(\"none\"));",["null","A NullPointerException is thrown","Optional.empty","none"],3,"ofNullable(null) is empty, map does nothing on an empty Optional, and orElse gives the fallback. Optional.of(null) would throw the NullPointerException.","Streams: Optional",["An empty Optional does not produce the text null; orElse supplies \"none\".","ofNullable accepts null and returns an empty Optional; only Optional.of(null) throws.","orElse unwraps the Optional, so the printed value is the fallback string, not Optional.empty.",null],1],["js68",0,6,"What happens?\nMap<Character, String> m = Stream.of(\"apple\", \"avocado\")\n  .collect(Collectors.toMap(s -> s.charAt(0), s -> s));",["m is {a=avocado}","m is {a=apple}","An IllegalStateException is thrown","It does not compile"],2,"Both keys are 'a' and no merge function was given, so toMap throws IllegalStateException for the duplicate key. A third argument such as (x, y) -> y would keep one value.","Streams: Collectors.toMap",["toMap without a merge function never picks a winner for a duplicate key; it throws.","toMap does not keep the first value for a duplicate key unless a merge function says so.",null,"The code is type-correct: charAt returns char, which boxes to Character."],2],["js69",0,7,"A framework must use reflection at runtime to set private fields of classes in package com.shop.model, but other modules should not compile against that package. What goes in module-info.java?",["opens com.shop.model;","requires com.shop.model;","exports com.shop.model;","uses com.shop.model;"],0,"opens grants deep reflective access at runtime only. exports gives compile-time access to public types and does not allow reflection on private members.","Modules: opens vs exports",[null,"requires names a module you depend on, not a package, and grants no reflective access.","exports allows compile-time use of public types, which is unwanted here, and does not allow deep reflection on private fields.","uses declares that a module consumes a service interface; it has nothing to do with reflection."],1],["js70",0,7,"Module app requires module api, and api's public methods return types from module util. What should api declare so that app can use those types without its own requires util?",["requires transitive util;","exports util;","requires static util;","opens util;"],0,"requires transitive makes every module that reads api also read util. requires static is for optional compile-time dependencies.","Modules: requires transitive",[null,"exports takes a package in the current module, not the name of another module.","requires static is an optional compile-time dependency and does not pass readability on to app.","opens takes a package and controls reflection; it cannot pass a module dependency to other modules."],1],["js71",0,7,"Module pay.stripe provides an implementation of the service interface com.pay.Gateway, and the app loads implementations with ServiceLoader. Which directive belongs in the provider module?",["uses com.pay.Gateway;","provides com.pay.Gateway with com.stripe.StripeGateway;","exports com.pay.Gateway to com.stripe;","requires service com.pay.Gateway;"],1,"The provider declares provides ... with ..., and the consumer module declares uses. \"requires service\" is not a directive.","Modules: services",["uses belongs in the consumer module that loads implementations with ServiceLoader, not in the provider.",null,"A qualified export shares a package with a named module; it does not register a service implementation.","requires service is not a real module directive."],1],["js72",0,7,"On Java 25, a class starts with `import module java.base;` and `import module java.desktop;` and declares a field `List<String> names;`. What happens?",["It compiles and List means java.util.List","It compiles and List means java.awt.List","It fails: List is ambiguous; add import java.util.List;","It fails because import module needs a module-info.java"],2,"Both modules export a public List type (java.util and java.awt), so the simple name is ambiguous. A single-type import takes priority and fixes it; import module also works in classes on the class path.","Modules: module import declarations",["java.desktop also exports java.awt.List, so the simple name List is ambiguous and not resolved to java.util.","Nothing gives java.awt.List priority; both on-demand module imports are equal, so the name is ambiguous.",null,"import module declarations work in ordinary class-path code without module-info.java."],3],["js73",0,7,"On Java 25, Hello.java contains only:\nvoid main() {\n  IO.println(\"Hello \" + List.of(1, 2));\n}\nWhat happens with `java Hello.java`?",["It prints Hello [1, 2]","It fails: main must be public static","It fails: no class declaration","It fails because List is not imported"],0,"A compact source file declares an implicit class, may use an instance main method, and automatically imports module java.base, so List needs no import. IO is java.lang.IO.","Packaging: compact source files and instance main",[null,"Java 25 allows an instance main method with no parameters and no public static modifiers.","A compact source file declares an implicit class, so no explicit class declaration is needed.","Compact source files automatically import module java.base, which includes java.util.List."],2],["js74",0,7,"A team wants to ship a desktop app with a small custom Java runtime that contains only the modules the app needs. Which JDK tool builds that runtime image?",["jlink","jdeps","jar","javadoc"],0,"jlink links the modules you choose into a custom runtime image. jdeps helps by listing which modules are needed, but it does not build the image.","Packaging: JDK tools",[null,"jdeps analyzes dependencies and can list the modules an app needs, but it does not build a runtime image.","jar packages class files into archives; it cannot create a Java runtime.","javadoc generates API documentation and has nothing to do with runtimes."],1],["js75",0,8,"A service calls a slow REST API for each of 50,000 incoming requests; each call spends most of its time waiting on the network. Which approach scales best on Java 25 with minimal code change?",["A fixed thread pool sized to the number of CPU cores","A parallel stream over the requests","Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()","One platform thread per request created with new Thread()"],2,"Virtual threads are cheap and park while blocked on I/O, so one per task scales to very large numbers of blocking tasks. A core-sized pool or parallel stream would leave most tasks waiting in line.","Concurrency: virtual threads",["A core-sized pool gives only a handful of threads, so most tasks queue while threads sit blocked on the network.","Parallel streams use the common ForkJoinPool, sized to the CPU cores, and suit CPU-bound work, not blocking I/O.",null,"Platform threads are costly OS threads, and creating 50,000 of them can exhaust memory or OS limits."],3],["js76",0,8,"Several threads run `hits++` on a shared int field, and the final total is often too low. What is the simplest correct fix?",["Mark the field volatile","Call Thread.yield() after each increment","Use an AtomicInteger and call incrementAndGet()","Give the threads a higher priority"],2,"hits++ is a read-modify-write, and AtomicInteger does it atomically. volatile only gives visibility, not atomicity, so updates can still be lost.","Concurrency: thread safety",["volatile makes writes visible to other threads but does not make the read-modify-write of ++ atomic.","yield is just a scheduling hint and does not stop two threads from interleaving an increment.",null,"Thread priority affects scheduling only and gives no atomicity or mutual exclusion."],1],["js77",0,8,"A task submitted to an ExecutorService must return a computed value and may throw a checked IOException. Which should it implement?",["Runnable","Supplier<V>","Thread","Callable<V>"],3,"Callable.call() returns a value and declares throws Exception, and the value is read through Future.get(). Runnable.run() and Supplier.get() cannot throw checked exceptions.","Concurrency: Callable and Future",["Runnable.run() returns void and cannot throw checked exceptions.","Supplier.get() cannot throw checked exceptions, and ExecutorService.submit does not take a Supplier.","Thread is a thread object to run, not a task type that returns a value.",null],1],["js78",0,8,"Many threads iterate over a list of listeners, while changes to the list are rare. Iteration must never throw ConcurrentModificationException. Which collection fits best?",["ArrayList","LinkedList","CopyOnWriteArrayList","Collections.unmodifiableList(new ArrayList<>())"],2,"CopyOnWriteArrayList copies the array on every write, so iterators work on a snapshot and never throw. It suits many reads and few writes. An unmodifiable wrapper does not allow changes at all.","Concurrency: concurrent collections",["ArrayList iterators are fail-fast and throw ConcurrentModificationException when another thread changes the list.","LinkedList is not thread-safe, and its iterators are fail-fast just like ArrayList's.",null,"An unmodifiable wrapper rejects every change, but the listener list still needs occasional updates."],2],["js79",0,8,"Called from the main method, what is printed?\nThread t = new Thread(() ->\n  System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()));\nt.setName(\"worker\");\nt.run();",["worker","main","Thread-0","Nothing"],1,"Calling run() directly runs the lambda in the current thread (main); only start() creates a new thread. It would print worker if start() were called.","Concurrency: thread lifecycle",["The name worker would appear only if start() ran the lambda on thread t.",null,"Thread-0 is the default name of a new thread, which is not the thread running the code; also t was renamed.","run() is a normal method call, so the lambda does run and print."],2],["js80",0,8,"On Java 25, a static final ScopedValue<String> USER is bound with `ScopedValue.where(USER, \"ann\").run(task)`. What is true?",["Inside task, USER.get() returns \"ann\"; after run returns, USER.get() throws NoSuchElementException","USER keeps \"ann\" for the rest of the thread's life, like a ThreadLocal","The task can call USER.set(\"bob\") to change the value","USER.get() returns null after run returns"],0,"A scoped value is bound only while run executes and cannot be changed within that scope. Outside any binding, get() throws NoSuchElementException. That limited lifetime is what separates it from ThreadLocal.","Concurrency: scoped values",[null,"Unlike ThreadLocal, a scoped value is bound only for the duration of run and unbound afterwards.","ScopedValue has no set method; a binding is immutable for its scope, and you rebind with a nested where().","Outside a binding, get() throws NoSuchElementException; it does not return null."],3],["js81",0,8,"Code uses `try (ExecutorService ex = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4)) { ... submit tasks ... }`. What happens at the end of the try block?",["close() shuts down the executor and waits for submitted tasks to finish","The code does not compile because ExecutorService is not AutoCloseable","All running tasks are interrupted and dropped at once","The executor keeps running until the JVM exits"],0,"ExecutorService has been AutoCloseable since Java 19, and close() calls shutdown() and then waits for tasks to finish. Interrupting and dropping tasks is what shutdownNow() does.","Concurrency: ExecutorService",[null,"ExecutorService has implemented AutoCloseable since Java 19, so it is valid in try-with-resources.","Interrupting running tasks is what shutdownNow() does; close() waits for them.","close() shuts the executor down, so its threads end once the submitted tasks finish."],2],["js82",0,8,"Two threads sometimes deadlock because each holds one lock and waits for the other. Which change most directly avoids waiting forever while keeping explicit locks?",["Use ReentrantLock.tryLock(timeout, unit) and back off if the lock isn't acquired","Add Thread.sleep() before each lock","Mark the shared fields volatile","Raise the priority of one thread"],0,"tryLock with a timeout lets a thread give up and retry instead of blocking forever. Another fix is always taking locks in the same order. Sleeping or changing priority only changes the timing.","Concurrency: locks and deadlock",[null,"Sleeping only changes timing; each thread can still end up waiting forever for the other's lock.","volatile controls visibility of fields and has nothing to do with lock acquisition.","Priority is only a scheduling hint and cannot break a cycle of threads waiting for each other's locks."],3],["js83",0,9,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(Path.of(\"/a/b\").resolve(\"../c\").normalize());",["/a/b/c","/a/c","/c","/a/b/../c"],1,"resolve gives /a/b/../c, and normalize removes b/.. to give /a/c. Without normalize the .. would stay in the path.","I/O: Path operations",["The .. segment removes b rather than adding a level, so the result is not under /a/b.",null,"The .. only goes up one level, from /a/b to /a, not to the root.","This is the result of resolve alone; normalize removes the b/.. part."],1],["js84",0,9,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(Path.of(\"/x/y\").relativize(Path.of(\"/x/z/w\")));",["z/w","/x/z/w","../z/w","../../z/w"],2,"relativize gives the path from /x/y to /x/z/w: up one level to /x, then down into z/w. z/w would be relative to /x, not /x/y.","I/O: Path operations",["z/w would be relative to /x; starting from /x/y, you first need .. to go up one level.","relativize returns a relative path, not the absolute target.",null,"Only one .. is needed, because /x/y and /x/z/w share the /x parent."],2],["js85",0,9,"A method counts lines containing \"ERROR\" using Files.lines(path).filter(...).count(). Over time the application runs out of file handles. What is the fix?",["Use Files.readString instead of Files.lines","Wrap the stream in try-with-resources so it is closed","Call System.gc() after each count","Use a parallel stream"],1,"Files.lines keeps the file open until the stream is closed, and a terminal operation does not close it. try-with-resources closes it every time.","I/O: Files and streams",["readString would load the whole file into memory and skips the real issue: an unclosed Files.lines stream holds a file handle.",null,"System.gc() does not reliably close file handles; resources must be closed explicitly.","A parallel stream does not close the file either, and it adds overhead."],2],["js86",0,9,"A Serializable User class has a field `transient String password;`. After a User is written with ObjectOutputStream and read back, what is the password field?",["The original value","An empty string","null","The field's value encrypted"],2,"transient fields are skipped during serialization and get their default value (null for references) on deserialization. They are not stored in any form.","I/O: serialization",["transient fields are skipped during serialization, so the original value is not restored.","Deserialization gives a transient reference field its default value, null, not an empty string.",null,"Java serialization does not encrypt anything; transient fields are not written at all."],1],["js87",0,9,"A tool must find every .log file under /var/app, including files in nested subfolders. Which Files method is designed for this?",["Files.list","Files.walk","Files.lines","Files.readAllLines"],1,"Files.walk goes through the whole tree recursively and returns a Stream<Path> to filter. Files.list only returns the direct children of one folder.","I/O: walking directories",["Files.list returns only the direct children of one directory and does not recurse.",null,"Files.lines reads the lines of one file, not the entries of a directory tree.","Files.readAllLines reads all lines of one file into a List; it does not list files."],1],["js88",0,9,"The folder /data does not exist. A program calls Files.createDirectory(Path.of(\"/data/reports/2026\")). What happens?",["All three folders are created","A NoSuchFileException is thrown because the parent is missing","Only 2026 is created, in the current directory","The call returns false"],1,"createDirectory makes only the last element and fails if the parent is missing. createDirectories would create all the missing folders.","I/O: Files methods",["Creating all missing parents is what Files.createDirectories does, not createDirectory.",null,"The absolute path is used as given; it is not re-rooted in the current directory.","Files.createDirectory reports failure by throwing an exception, not by returning a boolean (it returns the Path)."],1],["js89",0,10,"The default locale is en_US. The app has Messages.properties and Messages_fr.properties, and calls ResourceBundle.getBundle(\"Messages\", Locale.CANADA_FRENCH) (fr_CA). Which existing file is used first to look up a key?",["Messages.properties","Messages_en_US.properties","Messages_fr.properties","A MissingResourceException is thrown"],2,"There is no Messages_fr_CA, so lookup falls back to Messages_fr. Keys missing there come from its parent, Messages.properties. The default locale is only tried if no bundle is found for fr at all.","Localization: resource bundles",["The base bundle is only the parent used for keys missing from Messages_fr, not the first file searched.","The default locale is used only if no bundle is found for fr_CA or fr, and Messages_fr exists.",null,"Fallback finds Messages_fr and the base bundle, so the lookup does not fail."],3],["js90",0,10,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(NumberFormat.getCurrencyInstance(Locale.US).format(1234.5));",["1,234.50 USD","$1234.5","USD 1234.50","$1,234.50"],3,"The US currency format adds the dollar sign, grouping separators and two decimal places. The ISO code form is not the default for Locale.US.","Localization: NumberFormat",["The US currency instance uses the $ symbol before the number, not the ISO code after it.","The currency format adds grouping separators and always shows two decimal places for dollars.","The default Locale.US currency format shows the symbol $, not the ISO code USD.",null],1],["js91",0,10,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(\"dd MMM yyyy\", Locale.US)\n  .format(LocalDate.of(2026, 9, 24)));",["24 Sep 2026","24 09 2026","24 September 2026","Sep 24 2026"],0,"MMM is the short month name in the given locale, and dd and yyyy are the two-digit day and year. MMMM would give the full name September.","Localization: DateTimeFormatter",[null,"MMM is a text month abbreviation; numeric 09 would need the pattern MM.","The full month name needs MMMM; MMM gives the short form.","The pattern puts the day first, so the month cannot appear first."],1],["js92",0,10,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(NumberFormat.getCompactNumberInstance(\n  Locale.US, NumberFormat.Style.SHORT).format(1_200_000));",["1.2M","1 million","1M","1,200K"],2,"Compact formatting defaults to no fraction digits, so 1,200,000 becomes 1M. Call setMaximumFractionDigits(1) to get 1.2M, and use Style.LONG for 1 million.","Localization: CompactNumberFormat",["Compact formatting defaults to zero fraction digits, so 1.2M would need setMaximumFractionDigits(1).","The spelled-out form comes from Style.LONG; SHORT uses the M suffix.",null,"Compact format picks the largest fitting unit, so millions use M rather than K with grouping."],2],["x1",0,1,"What is the output?\nint a = 0b101 + 0x1F + 010;\nSystem.out.println(a);",["44","46","36","It does not compile"],0,"0b101 is binary 5, 0x1F is hex 31 and 010 is octal 8, so the sum is 44. 46 is the tempting answer if you read 010 as decimal ten, but a leading zero marks an octal literal.","Domain 1: Integer literals in binary, hex and octal",[null,"46 treats 010 as decimal ten, but a leading zero makes it an octal literal worth 8.","The octal literal 010 is 8, not 0, so it must be added to the total.","Binary (0b), hex (0x) and octal (leading 0) literals are all valid int literals."],2],["x2",0,1,"Which declaration fails to compile?",["int a = 1_000_000;","double d = 3_.14;","int b = 0b1010_1010;","long c = 9_999L;"],1,"An underscore may appear only between digits, so placing one next to the decimal point is a compile error. 0b1010_1010 can look suspicious, but underscores are allowed between binary digits too.","Domain 1: Underscores in numeric literals",["Underscores between digits are allowed, so 1_000_000 is a valid int literal.",null,"Underscores may separate binary digits, so 0b1010_1010 is valid.","An underscore between digits followed by the L suffix is valid."],1],["x3",0,1,"What is the output?\nLocalDate d = LocalDate.of(2025, 1, 31);\nSystem.out.println(Period.between(d, LocalDate.of(2025, 3, 1)));",["P29D","P1M","P1M1D","P30D"],2,"Period.between() counts whole months, where 31 January plus one month is 28 February 2025, and then the remaining days, one, giving P1M1D. P29D is tempting because it is the total day count, but Period keeps months separate.","Domain 1: Period calculations",["Period.between() reports whole months first and then leftover days, not only a day count.","After adding one month (to 28 February) one day remains, so the period is not exactly one month.",null,"There are 29 days between these dates, and the period is expressed in months and days anyway."],3],["x4",0,1,"A subscription page must show how many years, months and days remain between two LocalDate values. Which expression fits?",["Duration.between(start, end).toDays()","Instant.between(start, end)","start.compareTo(end)","Period.between(start, end)"],3,"Period models a date-based amount in years, months and days, which suits LocalDate values. Duration is tempting but measures seconds and nanoseconds, so it fails with dates that have no time part.","Domain 1: Period versus Duration",["Duration works with time-based units; with two LocalDate values it throws UnsupportedTemporalTypeException.","Instant has no between() method, and LocalDate values are not instants.","compareTo() only says which date is earlier; it does not give years, months and days.",null],2],["x5",0,1,"What is the output?\nString s = \"\\u2003 hi \\u2003\";\nSystem.out.println(s.trim().length() + \" \" + s.strip().length());",["2 2","6 6","6 2","2 6"],2,"trim() strips only characters whose code is at most U+0020, so the em spaces at both ends block it and the length stays 6, while strip() recognises Unicode whitespace and leaves hi. 2 2 is tempting if you treat the two methods as identical.","Domain 1: strip() versus trim()",["trim() removes only characters up to U+0020, so the em space (U+2003) at each end stops it.","strip() uses Unicode whitespace rules and does remove the em spaces, leaving hi.",null,"The results are reversed: trim() is the one that keeps the Unicode spaces."],3],["x6",0,1,"What is the output?\nStringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"0123456\");\nsb.delete(1, 3).replace(2, 4, \"X\");\nSystem.out.println(sb);",["0X56","03X56","0X3456","03X6"],3,"delete(1, 3) removes indexes 1 and 2 to give 03456, then replace(2, 4, \"X\") swaps indexes 2 and 3 (45) for X, giving 03X6. 03X56 is tempting if you treat the end index as inclusive of only one character.","Domain 1: StringBuilder delete and replace",["replace() works on the already shortened text 03456, so positions 2 to 4 hold 45, not 12.","The end index of replace() is exclusive, so both 4 and 5 are replaced by X.","delete(1, 3) removes 1 and 2 first, so 3 moves to index 1.",null],2],["x7",0,1,"What is the output?\nint i = 130;\nbyte b = (byte) i;\nSystem.out.println(b + \" \" + (char) 66);",["-126 B","130 B","-126 66","It does not compile"],0,"Narrowing keeps only the low 8 bits, so 130 wraps to -126, and (char) 66 is the character B. 130 B is tempting, but an explicit cast silently overflows rather than failing.","Domain 1: Narrowing conversions and casting",[null,"A byte holds only -128 to 127, so 130 cannot survive the narrowing cast.","Casting 66 to char produces the character B, and string concatenation prints it as B.","An explicit cast allows narrowing, so the code compiles."],2],["x8",0,2,"What is the output?\nint n = 15;\nString r = n % 3 == 0 ? n % 5 == 0 ? \"FB\" : \"F\" : \"N\";\nSystem.out.println(r);",["F","FB","N","It does not compile"],1,"The conditional operator groups to the right, so the expression reads n % 3 == 0 ? (n % 5 == 0 ? \"FB\" : \"F\") : \"N\", and 15 passes both tests. F is tempting if you stop after the first test.","Domain 2: The ternary operator",["15 is also divisible by 5, so the inner ternary picks FB, not F.",null,"15 is divisible by 3, so the outer condition is true and N is not chosen.","Nested ternary operators without parentheses are legal and group to the right."],2],["x9",0,2,"Which statement, placed inside a method, fails to compile?",["if (false) { System.out.println(); }","while (false) { System.out.println(); }","do { } while (false);","for (int i = 0; i < 0; i++) { }"],1,"A while loop whose condition is the constant false makes its body unreachable, which is a compile error. if (false) looks equivalent, but the language makes a special exception for if so that it can be used for conditional compilation.","Domain 2: Unreachable code",["The language deliberately allows if (false) so code can be switched off, so it compiles.",null,"A do-while body runs at least once, so it is reachable and compiles.","i < 0 is not a constant expression, so the compiler treats the body as reachable."],3],["x10",0,2,"A method declares `int x;`, then runs `if (flag) x = 1;` where flag is a boolean parameter, and then `System.out.println(x);`. What happens?",["It does not compile: x might not be initialized","It prints 0 when flag is false","It prints 1 in every case","A NullPointerException when flag is false"],0,"Local variables must be definitely assigned before use, and the compiler sees a path where x is never set. Printing 0 is tempting because fields default to 0, but local variables have no default.","Domain 2: Definite assignment",[null,"Local variables get no default value, so there is no 0 to print.","x is assigned only when flag is true, and the compiler does not assume that.","An int is a primitive and cannot be null; the problem is caught at compile time."],2],["x11",0,2,"What is the output?\nrecord P(int x, int y) {}\n// in main:\nObject o = new P(2, 3);\nString s = switch (o) {\n  case P(int x, int y) when x > y -> \"A\";\n  case P(var x, var y) -> \"B\" + (x + y);\n  default -> \"C\";\n};\nSystem.out.println(s);",["A","B23","C","B5"],3,"The guarded case fails because 2 is not greater than 3, and the second record pattern binds x and y, adding them in parentheses before concatenation. B23 is tempting, but the parentheses force numeric addition.","Domain 2: Record patterns and when guards",["The guard x > y is false for 2 and 3, so the first case does not match.","The parentheses make x + y an int addition before concatenation, giving 5.","The second record pattern matches any P, so default is not reached.",null],3],["x12",0,2,"What is the output?\nint[] a = {1, 2, 3};\nfor (int v : a) v *= 2;\nSystem.out.println(Arrays.toString(a));",["[2, 4, 6]","[2, 2, 3]","[1, 2, 3]","It does not compile"],2,"The enhanced for loop copies each element into v, so changing v leaves the array untouched. [2, 4, 6] is tempting, but updating an array needs an indexed loop such as a[i] *= 2.","Domain 2: Enhanced for loops",["v is a copy of each element, so doubling it does not change the array.","No element of the array is changed, including the first.",null,"Assigning to the enhanced for variable is legal; it just has no effect on the array."],2],["x13",0,3,"What does `new B();` print?\nclass A {\n  static { System.out.print(\"s\"); }\n  { System.out.print(\"a\"); }\n  A() { System.out.print(\"A\"); }\n}\nclass B extends A {\n  static { System.out.print(\"t\"); }\n  { System.out.print(\"b\"); }\n  B() { System.out.print(\"B\"); }\n}",["stAaBb","saAtbB","tsaAbB","staAbB"],3,"Static initializers run first, parent then child (s, t); then for the object, A's instance initializer and constructor run (a, A) before B's (b, B). saAtbB is tempting, but both classes are initialized before the object is created.","Domain 3: Initialization order with inheritance",["Instance initializer blocks run before the constructor body of the same class.","Both classes are initialized, parent first, before any instance code runs.","A superclass is initialized before its subclass, so s comes before t.",null],3],["x14",0,3,"What is the output?\nrecord Temp(double c) {\n  Temp {\n    if (c < -273.15) throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n    c = Math.round(c);\n  }\n}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(new Temp(21.6).c());",["21.6","22","22.0","It does not compile: c cannot be reassigned"],2,"A compact constructor works on its implicit parameters, and the fields are assigned from them at the end, so the rounded value is stored as the double 22.0. The compile error is tempting because record fields are final, but it is the parameter being changed.","Domain 3: Records and compact constructors",["In a compact constructor the parameter can be reassigned, and its final value is stored in the field.","c is a double component, so the rounded long is widened back and prints as 22.0.",null,"Compact constructor parameters are not final; reassigning them is the intended way to normalize values."],3],["x15",0,3,"Given `interface Util { static String tag() { return \"U\"; } }` and `class Impl implements Util {}`, which call compiles?",["Impl.tag()","Util.tag()","new Impl().tag()","Util.super.tag()"],1,"Static methods in interfaces are not inherited, so they must be called through the interface name. Impl.tag() is tempting because static methods of superclasses are inherited, but interfaces follow a different rule.","Domain 3: Static interface methods",["Static interface methods are not inherited by implementing classes, so Impl.tag() is not found.",null,"An object of an implementing class cannot call the interface's static method.","Interface.super syntax calls default methods from an implementing class, not static methods."],2],["x16",0,3,"Given `class Animal {}`, `class Dog extends Animal {}` and `class Cat extends Animal {}`, what happens with:\nAnimal a = new Dog();\nCat c = (Cat) a;",["It compiles, then throws ClassCastException","It fails to compile: Dog cannot be cast to Cat","It compiles, and c refers to a Dog object","It compiles, and c is set to null"],0,"Downcasting from Animal to Cat compiles because an Animal variable could hold a Cat, but at run time the object is a Dog, so ClassCastException is thrown. The compile error is tempting, but the compiler does not know the object's real class.","Domain 3: Polymorphism and casting",[null,"The compiler checks only the declared type Animal, which could hold a Cat, so the cast is allowed.","The JVM checks the real object's class at run time and refuses to treat a Dog as a Cat.","A failed cast throws an exception; it never produces null."],2],["x17",0,3,"What happens?\nint base = 10;\nRunnable r = new Runnable() {\n  public void run() { System.out.println(base + 1); }\n};\nbase = 20;\nr.run();",["It prints 11","It does not compile: base is not effectively final","It prints 21","It throws IllegalStateException"],1,"Local and anonymous classes, like lambdas, may use local variables only if they are final or effectively final, and base = 20 breaks that. Printing 11 is tempting if you think the value is copied at creation, but the compiler rejects the code.","Domain 3: Anonymous classes and captured variables",["base is reassigned, so it is not effectively final and the anonymous class cannot capture it.",null,"The value captured would never be the later 20, and in any case the code does not compile.","This is a compile-time rule, not a run-time check."],2],["x18",0,3,"`class A { Number get() { return 0; } }`. Which method in `class B extends A` is a valid override?",["Object get() { return 1; }","int get() { return 1; }","long get() { return 1L; }","Integer get() { return 1; }"],3,"Overriding allows covariant return types, so a subclass may return Integer, a subclass of Number. int is tempting because of autoboxing, but return types in overriding must be reference subtypes.","Domain 3: Overriding and covariant returns",["The return type may be narrowed, not widened; Object is a supertype of Number.","A primitive int is not a subtype of Number, so this is an incompatible return type.","long is a primitive, and a primitive return type cannot override a reference type.",null],2],["x19",0,3,"What is the output?\nenum Op {\n  ADD { int apply(int a, int b) { return a + b; } },\n  MUL { int apply(int a, int b) { return a * b; } };\n  abstract int apply(int a, int b);\n}\n// in main:\nint r = 0;\nfor (Op op : Op.values()) r += op.apply(2, 3);\nSystem.out.println(r);",["11","5","6","It does not compile: enums cannot be abstract"],0,"Each constant has its own class body implementing apply(), and values() visits ADD then MUL, giving 5 + 6 = 11. The compile error is tempting, but abstract methods are allowed when every constant implements them.","Domain 3: Enums with methods",[null,"values() returns every constant, so MUL's result is added too.","ADD also runs, contributing 5 before MUL adds 6.","An enum may declare an abstract method as long as every constant supplies a body."],3],["x20",0,3,"`sealed class Vehicle permits Car, Truck {}`. Third-party code must be able to extend Truck freely. How should Truck be declared?",["final class Truck extends Vehicle {}","sealed class Truck extends Vehicle {}","non-sealed class Truck extends Vehicle {}","class Truck extends Vehicle {}"],2,"non-sealed reopens the hierarchy below Truck, letting any class extend it while Vehicle stays sealed. A plain class is tempting, but a permitted subclass must choose final, sealed or non-sealed.","Domain 3: Sealed classes and non-sealed",["final stops every subclass, which is the opposite of what is needed.","A sealed Truck would itself restrict its subclasses to a permitted list.",null,"A permitted subclass must say final, sealed or non-sealed, so this does not compile."],2],["x21",0,3,"Given `Object o`, which condition fails to compile?",["o instanceof String s && s.isEmpty()","!(o instanceof String s) || s.isEmpty()","o instanceof String s || s.isEmpty()","o instanceof String s ? s.isEmpty() : false"],2,"With ||, the right side runs only when the instanceof test failed, so the pattern variable s is not definitely matched there and is out of scope. The negated form is tempting to reject, but there s is in scope because the right side runs only after a match.","Domain 3: instanceof patterns and flow scoping",["With &&, the right side runs only when the match succeeded, so s is in scope.","The right side of || runs only when the left is false, which means the match succeeded, so s is in scope.",null,"The true branch of the conditional runs only after a successful match, so s is in scope."],3],["x22",0,3,"What is the output?\nclass A {}\nclass B extends A {}\nstatic String m(A a) { return \"A\"; }\nstatic String m(B b) { return \"B\"; }\n// in main:\nA x = new B();\nSystem.out.println(m(x));",["A","B","AB","It does not compile: the call is ambiguous"],0,"Overload resolution happens at compile time using the static type of the argument, which is A, so m(A) is chosen. B is tempting because the object is a B, but only overriding uses the run-time type.","Domain 3: Overloading versus overriding",[null,"Overloads are chosen at compile time from the declared type A, not the object's run-time class.","Only one overload runs for a single call.","Only m(A) accepts an expression of type A, so there is no ambiguity."],3],["x23",0,3,"What is the output?\ninterface I { default String who() { return \"I\"; } }\nclass P { public String who() { return \"P\"; } }\nclass C extends P implements I {}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(new C().who());",["I","PI","It does not compile: who() is inherited twice","P"],3,"When a class inherits a concrete method from its superclass and a default method with the same signature from an interface, the class method wins. The compile error is tempting because two defaults would conflict, but a superclass method is not a default.","Domain 3: Default methods and the class-wins rule",["A method inherited from a class takes priority over an interface default method.","Only one who() runs; nothing combines the two results.","The class-wins rule resolves the conflict, so C needs no override.",null],3],["x24",0,3,"What is the output?\nrecord R(int[] a) {}\n// in main:\nint[] arr = {1};\nSystem.out.println(new R(arr).equals(new R(arr)) + \" \"\n  + new R(new int[]{1}).equals(new R(new int[]{1})));",["true true","true false","false false","false true"],1,"A record's generated equals() compares reference components with their equals(), and arrays use identity, so the shared array is equal but two separate arrays are not. true true is tempting if you expect records to compare array contents.","Domain 3: Records and equals()",["Record equals() compares an array component with the array's own equals(), which is identity, so equal contents do not count.",null,"Two records holding the very same array reference are equal, so the first result is true.","The first pair shares one array, so it is equal; only the second pair differs."],3],["x25",0,4,"What does `System.out.println(f());` print?\nstatic int f() {\n  try { throw new RuntimeException(); }\n  finally { return 2; }\n}",["A RuntimeException reaches the caller","Nothing: it does not compile (missing return)","2, followed by a stack trace","2"],3,"When finally completes abruptly with return, the exception thrown in try is discarded and the method returns 2. Expecting the RuntimeException to propagate is tempting, which is exactly why returning from finally is considered bad practice.","Domain 4: try/finally flow",["A return in finally discards the pending exception, so it never reaches the caller.","The finally block always returns, so the method does complete with a value and compiles.","The exception is discarded completely, so no stack trace is printed.",null],3],["x26",0,4,"A recursive method recurses too deeply inside `try { ... } catch (Exception e) { log(e); }`. What happens?",["The catch block logs it as an Exception","The StackOverflowError is not caught and propagates","The JVM retries the call with a larger stack","The error is converted to a RuntimeException"],1,"StackOverflowError is a subclass of Error, which sits beside Exception under Throwable, so catch (Exception e) does not catch it. Expecting the catch block to log it is tempting because it looks like a catch-all.","Domain 4: The Throwable hierarchy",["StackOverflowError extends Error, not Exception, so catch (Exception e) does not match it.",null,"The JVM never retries a call when the stack overflows.","Errors are not wrapped automatically; the StackOverflowError itself propagates."],2],["x27",0,4,"Why does this fail to compile?\ncatch (IOException | SQLException e) {\n  e = new IOException(\"retry\");\n}",["IOException and SQLException are related types","A multi-catch block must rethrow the exception","A multi-catch parameter is implicitly final","SQLException is unchecked, so it cannot be caught"],2,"The parameter of a multi-catch clause is implicitly final, so assigning a new value to e is a compile error. The related-types rule is tempting, but it only applies when one type is a subclass of the other, which is not the case here.","Domain 4: Multi-catch rules",["SQLException extends Exception, not IOException, so the two types are unrelated and may share a catch.","Rethrowing is optional; the block can handle the exception and continue.",null,"SQLException is a checked exception, and checked exceptions can be caught normally."],2],["x28",0,4,"`BufferedReader br = Files.newBufferedReader(p);` is declared earlier and never reassigned. Which try-with-resources header compiles?",["try (br) { ... }","try (BufferedReader copy) { ... }","try (Files.newBufferedReader(p)) { ... }","try (var copy) { ... }"],0,"Since Java 9, a try-with-resources header can name an existing final or effectively final variable, so try (br) is valid. try (Files.newBufferedReader(p)) is tempting, but only variable names or declarations with initializers are allowed.","Domain 4: try-with-resources",[null,"A resource declaration must include an initializer, so a bare declaration is rejected.","A resource must be a declaration or a variable name; an arbitrary method call is not allowed.","var needs an initializer to infer the type, and a resource needs one anyway."],3],["x29",0,4,"Files.readString(Path) declares IOException. What happens with this code in a method that has no throws clause?\npaths.forEach(p -> System.out.println(Files.readString(p)));",["It does not compile: the lambda cannot throw IOException","It compiles; forEach wraps it in UncheckedIOException","It compiles; the exception is silently ignored","It compiles, but only as a parallel stream"],0,"Consumer.accept() does not declare checked exceptions, so a lambda that may throw IOException must catch it itself. Expecting forEach to wrap it in UncheckedIOException is tempting, but that wrapping must be written by hand.","Domain 4: Checked exceptions and lambdas",[null,"forEach does no wrapping; UncheckedIOException must be thrown by your own code.","Checked exceptions are never silently ignored; the compiler requires them to be handled.","Parallel execution does not change the compile-time rules for checked exceptions."],3],["x30",0,5,"What is the output?\nint[] a = {2, 4, 6, 8};\nSystem.out.println(Arrays.binarySearch(a, 6) + \" \" + Arrays.binarySearch(a, 5));",["2 -1","3 -3","2 -3","2 -2"],2,"binarySearch returns the index of a found key (6 at index 2), and for a missing key returns -(insertion point) - 1; 5 would go at index 2, so the result is -3. -2 is tempting if you forget the extra -1.","Domain 5: Arrays.binarySearch",["-1 means the key would go at index 0, but 5 belongs at index 2.","Index 2 holds 6, so the first search returns 2, not 3.",null,"A missing key gives -(insertion point) - 1, so index 2 yields -3, not -2."],3],["x31",0,5,"What is the output?\nint[] x = {1, 2, 3}, y = {1, 2, 4};\nSystem.out.println(Arrays.mismatch(x, y) + \" \" + Arrays.compare(x, y));",["3 -1","2 -1","2 1","-1 -1"],1,"mismatch() returns the first index where the arrays differ, 2, and compare() compares that pair, 3 and 4, returning a negative value. -1 for mismatch is tempting, but that value means no mismatch at all.","Domain 5: Arrays.mismatch and Arrays.compare",["mismatch() returns a zero-based index, so the third element is index 2.",null,"x has the smaller element (3 against 4) at the first difference, so compare() is negative.","-1 from mismatch() means the arrays are equal, which they are not."],2],["x32",0,5,"What is the output?\nMap<String, List<Integer>> m = new HashMap<>();\nm.computeIfAbsent(\"a\", k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(1);\nm.computeIfAbsent(\"a\", k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(2);\nSystem.out.println(m);",["{a=[2]}","{a=[1]}","{}","{a=[1, 2]}"],3,"computeIfAbsent() creates and stores a list only when the key is missing, and it returns the stored list both times, so both values end up in it. {a=[2]} is tempting if you think the list is replaced on each call.","Domain 5: Map.computeIfAbsent",["The second call finds the existing list and returns it, so no new list replaces it.","The second call adds 2 to the same list that already holds 1.","computeIfAbsent() stores the new list in the map before returning it.",null],2],["x33",0,5,"What is the output?\nMap<String, Integer> m = new HashMap<>();\nm.put(\"x\", null);\nm.putIfAbsent(\"x\", 5);\nSystem.out.println(m.get(\"x\") + \" \" + m.getOrDefault(\"y\", 0));",["5 0","null 0","5 null","null null"],0,"putIfAbsent() replaces a null value as if the key were missing, so x maps to 5, and getOrDefault() returns 0 for the missing key y. null 0 is tempting because the key x already existed.","Domain 5: putIfAbsent and getOrDefault",[null,"putIfAbsent() treats a key mapped to null as absent, so it stores 5.","getOrDefault() returns the default 0 because y has no mapping.","Both parts differ: x now maps to 5 and y falls back to 0."],3],["x34",0,5,"Inside `static void fill(List<? super Integer> list)`, which statement compiles?",["Integer i = list.get(0);","list.add(42);","Number n = list.get(0);","list.add(4.2);"],1,"With ? super Integer, the list accepts Integer values, but reading gives only Object because the element type could be Number or Object. Number n = list.get(0) is tempting, but the lower bound says nothing about what the list holds.","Domain 5: Lower-bounded wildcards",["The list might be a List<Object>, so get() returns Object, which cannot be assigned to Integer.",null,"The list might be a List<Object>, so its elements are not guaranteed to be Numbers.","A Double is not an Integer, and the list might be a List<Integer>."],3],["x35",0,5,"What happens when this line runs?\nSet<String> s = Set.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"a\");",["s contains a and b","It does not compile","IllegalArgumentException is thrown","s contains a, b and a"],2,"Set.of() throws IllegalArgumentException when given duplicate elements. Ending up with a and b is tempting because that is what adding to a HashSet does, but the factory method is stricter.","Domain 5: Unmodifiable collections",["Set.of() rejects duplicate elements instead of silently dropping them.","The duplicate is detected only at run time, so the code compiles.",null,"No Set implementation keeps duplicates, and Set.of() fails anyway."],2],["x36",0,6,"Which functional interface matches the lambda `() -> LocalDate.now()`, which takes no arguments and returns a value?",["Consumer<LocalDate>","Function<Void, LocalDate>","Predicate<LocalDate>","Supplier<LocalDate>"],3,"Supplier<T> has get(), which takes no arguments and returns a T, exactly the shape of this lambda. Function<Void, LocalDate> is tempting, but its apply() still requires one parameter.","Domain 6: Functional interfaces",["A Consumer takes an argument and returns nothing, which is the opposite shape.","Function.apply() takes one argument, but this lambda takes none.","A Predicate takes an argument and returns a boolean.",null],1],["x37",0,6,"Which declaration compiles with the method reference String::length?",["Supplier<Integer> f = String::length;","Predicate<String> f = String::length;","Function<String, Integer> f = String::length;","UnaryOperator<String> f = String::length;"],2,"String::length used without a receiver takes a String and returns its length, which fits Function<String, Integer> through autoboxing. Supplier<Integer> is tempting, but it would need a bound receiver such as \"abc\"::length.","Domain 6: Method references",["An unbound instance method reference needs the String as an argument, and Supplier.get() takes none.","length() returns an int, not a boolean, so it cannot implement test().",null,"UnaryOperator<String> must return a String, but length() returns an int."],2],["x38",0,6,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(Stream.iterate(1, n -> n < 20, n -> n * 3).toList());",["[1, 3, 9, 27]","[3, 9]","[3, 9, 27]","[1, 3, 9]"],3,"The three-argument iterate() starts with the seed, stops as soon as the predicate fails, and applies the operator between elements, giving 1, 3 and 9. Including 27 is tempting, but it is tested before being emitted.","Domain 6: Stream.iterate",["27 fails the test n < 20, so the stream ends before it.","The seed 1 is the first element; iterate() does not skip it.","The seed is included and 27 is excluded by the hasNext test.",null],2],["x39",0,6,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(Stream.of(5, 3, 5, 1, 3, 9)\n  .distinct().sorted().limit(3).toList());",["[1, 3, 5]","[1, 3, 3]","[5, 3, 1]","[1, 1, 3]"],0,"distinct() leaves 5, 3, 1, 9, sorted() orders them as 1, 3, 5, 9, and limit(3) keeps the first three. [5, 3, 1] is tempting if you forget that sorted() comes before limit().","Domain 6: Intermediate operations",[null,"distinct() removes the repeated 3, so it cannot appear twice.","sorted() runs before limit(), so the result is in ascending order.","There is only one 1 in the source, and duplicates are removed anyway."],2],["x40",0,6,"What is the output?\nstatic String fb() { System.out.print(\"F\"); return \"d\"; }\n// in main:\nString r = Optional.of(\"v\").orElse(fb())\n  + Optional.of(\"w\").orElseGet(() -> fb());\nSystem.out.println(r);",["vw","Fvw","FFvw","Fvd"],1,"An argument to orElse() is evaluated before the call even when a value is present, so F is printed once, while orElseGet() runs its supplier only for an empty Optional. vw is tempting if you assume orElse() is lazy.","Domain 6: Optional orElse versus orElseGet",["orElse() takes an already evaluated argument, so fb() runs and prints F even though v is present.",null,"orElseGet() calls its supplier only when the Optional is empty, so fb() runs only once.","The second Optional holds w, so its value is used rather than the fallback d."],3],["x41",0,6,"What is the output?\nIntSummaryStatistics st = IntStream.of(4, 8, 6).summaryStatistics();\nSystem.out.println(st.getMin() + \" \" + st.getAverage() + \" \" + st.getCount());",["4 6 3","4 6.0 3","4 6.0 18","8 6.0 3"],1,"IntSummaryStatistics reports min as an int, average as a double and count as a long, so the line is 4 6.0 3. 4 6 3 is tempting, but the average is always a double.","Domain 6: Primitive streams and summary statistics",["getAverage() returns a double, so it prints as 6.0.",null,"getCount() returns the number of elements, 3, not their sum.","getMin() returns the smallest value, 4; 8 is the maximum."],2],["x42",0,6,"What is the output?\nMap<Character, Integer> m = Stream.of(\"apple\", \"avocado\", \"banana\")\n  .collect(Collectors.toMap(s -> s.charAt(0), String::length, Integer::sum));\nSystem.out.println(m.get('a'));",["12","7","5","An IllegalStateException is thrown"],0,"The third argument to toMap() resolves duplicate keys, so apple (5) and avocado (7) both map to 'a' and are summed to 12. The exception is tempting because it happens without a merge function.","Domain 6: Collectors.toMap with a merge function",[null,"The merge function sums the values; it does not keep the last one.","The merge function combines both a-words rather than keeping the first.","With a merge function, duplicate keys are combined instead of causing an exception."],2],["x43",0,6,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(Stream.of(1, 2, 3, 4)\n  .gather(Gatherers.windowSliding(3)).toList());",["[[1, 2, 3], [4]]","[[1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4]]","[[1, 2, 3]]","[[1, 2, 3], [2, 3, 4]]"],3,"windowSliding(3) emits overlapping windows of three elements, moving one step each time, so four elements give two windows. [[1, 2, 3], [4]] is tempting, but that is what windowFixed(3) produces.","Domain 6: Stream gatherers",["That is windowFixed(3); a sliding window overlaps and moves by one element.","The window size is 3, so each window holds three elements.","The window slides once more to cover 2, 3, 4.",null],2],["x44",0,6,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(Stream.of(1, 2, 3)\n  .gather(Gatherers.scan(() -> 0, Integer::sum)).toList());",["[0, 1, 3, 6]","[6]","[1, 3, 6]","[1, 2, 3]"],2,"Gatherers.scan() emits a running result after each element, starting from the initial value 0, giving 1, 3 and 6. [6] is tempting, but that is the single result produced by Gatherers.fold().","Domain 6: Stream gatherers",["scan() does not emit the initial value on its own; it emits after combining each element.","A single final result is what Gatherers.fold() gives, not scan().",null,"scan() emits running totals, not the original elements."],3],["x45",0,6,"`List<Integer> out = new ArrayList<>(); IntStream.range(0, 10_000).parallel().forEach(out::add);` sometimes leaves fewer than 10,000 elements or throws. What is the BEST fix?",["Declare out as final","Declare out as a volatile field","Replace the ArrayList with a LinkedList","Collect instead: IntStream.range(0, 10_000).parallel().boxed().toList()"],3,"Adding to a shared non-thread-safe list from a parallel stream is a stateful side effect; letting the stream collect its own result avoids the race. volatile is tempting, but it only affects the reference, not the list's internal state.","Domain 6: Parallel streams and side effects",["final stops reassigning the variable but does nothing to make ArrayList thread-safe.","volatile affects visibility of the reference, not concurrent add() calls on the list.","LinkedList is no more thread-safe than ArrayList, so the race remains.",null],3],["x46",0,7,"A plain JAR named commons-text-1.10.jar, with no module-info and no Automatic-Module-Name manifest entry, is placed on the module path. What module name does it get?",["commons-text","commons.text.1.10","commons.text","It joins the unnamed module"],2,"An automatic module's name comes from the JAR file name with the version and .jar removed and non-alphanumeric characters replaced by dots, giving commons.text. The unnamed module is tempting, but that applies only to the class path.","Domain 7: Automatic modules",["Hyphens are not legal in module names, so they are replaced with dots.","The trailing version is removed from the file name before the name is derived.",null,"JARs on the module path become automatic modules; the unnamed module is for the class path."],3],["x47",0,7,"Code in a named module needs types from a JAR that is on the class path, not the module path. What happens?",["It works: the unnamed module exports all its packages","Compilation fails: named modules cannot read the unnamed module","It works once the module adds requires unnamed;","It works only after the app is packaged with jlink"],1,"Class-path code lives in the unnamed module, which named modules cannot declare a dependency on, so the types are not accessible. The first distractor is tempting because the unnamed module does export all its packages, but only other unnamed and automatic modules read it.","Domain 7: Module path versus class path",["The unnamed module does export everything, but a named module has no way to read it.",null,"The unnamed module has no name, so it cannot appear in a requires directive.","jlink links named modules into an image; it cannot fix a missing module dependency."],3],["x48",0,7,"Before modularizing a legacy JAR, a team wants a report of which packages and JDK modules it depends on, including JDK internal APIs. Which tool is designed for this?",["jdeps","jlink","jshell","jcmd"],0,"jdeps analyses class files and JARs and lists their package and module dependencies, and can flag uses of JDK internals. jlink is tempting because it also works with modules, but it assembles runtime images.","Domain 7: JDK tools",[null,"jlink builds a custom runtime image; it does not analyse a JAR's dependencies.","jshell is an interactive shell for trying Java code snippets.","jcmd sends diagnostic commands to a running JVM."],1],["x49",0,7,"Modules are compiled into the mods folder. Which command runs main class com.shop.App in module shop?",["java --module-path mods --module com.shop.App/shop","java --module-path mods --module shop/com.shop.App","java -cp mods shop/com.shop.App","java --module-path mods com.shop.App"],1,"The --module (or -m) option takes module/mainclass, and --module-path (or -p) says where the modules are. Swapping the order is the tempting slip, but the module name always comes first.","Domain 7: Running modules",["The module name comes before the slash and the class name after it.",null,"-cp puts the folder on the class path, and module/class syntax is not a class name.","Without --module the launcher looks for com.shop.App on the class path, not in the module."],2],["x50",0,8,"What happens?\nThread t = new Thread(() -> {});\nt.start();\nt.start();",["The task runs twice on the same thread","The second call is silently ignored","It does not compile","The second start() throws IllegalThreadStateException"],3,"A thread can be started only once, and calling start() again throws IllegalThreadStateException. Expecting the task to run twice is tempting, but a new Thread object is needed for each run.","Domain 8: Thread lifecycle",["A Thread object can be started only once; it cannot run its task a second time.","The second start() is detected and rejected with an exception, not ignored.","The code is valid Java; the problem is found only at run time.",null],2],["x51",0,8,"Which statement starts a new virtual thread running task?",["Thread.ofVirtual().start(task);","new VirtualThread(task).start();","Thread.ofPlatform().virtual().start(task);","Executors.newFixedThreadPool(1).submit(task);"],0,"Thread.ofVirtual() returns a builder for virtual threads, and start(task) creates and starts one. The fixed thread pool is tempting because it runs the task, but its workers are platform threads.","Domain 8: Virtual threads and Thread.Builder",[null,"There is no public VirtualThread class to instantiate; virtual threads come from Thread builders or factories.","The platform builder creates platform threads and has no virtual() method.","A fixed thread pool uses platform threads, not virtual threads."],1],["x52",0,8,"Class Counter has `synchronized void inc()` and `static synchronized void reset()`. Thread 1 calls c.inc() while Thread 2 calls Counter.reset(). What is true?",["They block each other because both are synchronized","reset() waits until every Counter object is unlocked","They can run at the same time because they lock different objects","It does not compile: a class cannot mix both kinds"],2,"An instance synchronized method locks this, while a static synchronized method locks Counter.class, so the two can run concurrently. Expecting them to block each other is tempting, which is why shared data touched by both needs a common lock.","Domain 8: synchronized methods",["Being synchronized in the same class is not enough; they must use the same monitor to block each other.","A static synchronized method locks only the Class object, not every instance.",null,"A class may freely declare both static and instance synchronized methods."],3],["x53",0,8,"A ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> named cache receives `cache.put(\"k\", null);`. What happens?",["The key is stored with a null value","Any existing mapping for k is removed","A NullPointerException is thrown","It does not compile"],2,"ConcurrentHashMap rejects null keys and values, so put() throws NullPointerException. Storing a null value is tempting because HashMap allows it, but a null would be ambiguous with a missing key in concurrent code.","Domain 8: Concurrent collections",["Unlike HashMap, ConcurrentHashMap does not allow null keys or values.","put() does not remove a key; this call fails before changing the map.",null,"The generic types accept null at compile time, so the problem appears only at run time."],2],["x54",0,8,"Two threads keep reacting to each other by releasing and retrying locks, so both stay busy but neither completes its work. What is this called?",["Livelock","Deadlock","Starvation","Race condition"],0,"In a livelock threads stay active and keep changing state in response to each other but make no progress. Deadlock is tempting, but deadlocked threads are blocked, not busy.","Domain 8: Deadlock, starvation and livelock",[null,"In a deadlock the threads are blocked and waiting, not actively retrying.","Starvation is one thread being denied resources while others progress.","A race condition is a wrong result caused by unsynchronized access to shared data."],1],["x55",0,9,"What is the output?\nPath p = Path.of(\"/home/ann/docs/a.txt\");\nSystem.out.println(p.getFileName() + \" \" + p.getParent().getFileName() + \" \" + p.getNameCount());",["a.txt docs 5","a.txt /home/ann/docs 4","a.txt ann 4","a.txt docs 4"],3,"getFileName() returns the last element, the parent is /home/ann/docs whose file name is docs, and the root is not counted, so there are 4 names. 5 is tempting if you count the root as an element.","Domain 9: Path operations",["The root / is not a name element, so there are four names, not five.","getFileName() on the parent returns only its last element, docs.","The parent is /home/ann/docs, whose last element is docs, not ann.",null],2],["x56",0,9,"A program calls Files.delete(Path.of(\"reports\")) on a directory that still contains files. What happens?",["The directory and its contents are deleted","DirectoryNotEmptyException is thrown","The call returns false","Only the files inside are deleted"],1,"Files.delete() removes only empty directories and throws DirectoryNotEmptyException otherwise. Returning false is tempting because the older File.delete() works that way, but the NIO method throws.","Domain 9: Files methods",["Files.delete() never deletes recursively; a directory must be empty first.",null,"Files.delete() returns void and reports failure with an exception.","Nothing is deleted; the call fails as a whole."],2],["x57",0,9,"A program calls Files.move(src, dst) with no options, and dst already exists. What happens, and how can it overwrite dst instead?",["dst is overwritten silently; no option is needed","It returns false; pass StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE","src is renamed to dst(1); pass LinkOption.NOFOLLOW_LINKS","FileAlreadyExistsException; pass StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING"],3,"Files.move() throws FileAlreadyExistsException if the target exists, unless REPLACE_EXISTING is given. ATOMIC_MOVE is tempting, but it only requires the move to happen as one atomic file system operation.","Domain 9: Files.copy and Files.move",["Without REPLACE_EXISTING, move() refuses to overwrite an existing target.","move() reports failure with an exception, and ATOMIC_MOVE makes the move atomic, not overwriting.","move() never invents a new name, and NOFOLLOW_LINKS is about symbolic links.",null],2],["x58",0,9,"A loop reads a text file with BufferedReader.readLine(). How does it detect the end of the file?",["readLine() returns an empty string","readLine() returns null","readLine() throws EOFException","readLine() returns -1"],1,"readLine() returns null when no more lines are available, so the usual loop is while ((line = br.readLine()) != null). -1 is tempting, but that is what the single-character read() method returns.","Domain 9: BufferedReader",["An empty string means a blank line, not the end of the file.",null,"readLine() signals end of stream by its return value, not an exception.","readLine() returns a String, so it cannot return the int -1; that is read()."],1],["x59",0,10,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(MessageFormat.format(\"{1} owes {0}\", \"Ann\", \"Bob\"));",["Ann owes Bob","{1} owes Ann","Bob owes Ann","Bob owes {0}"],2,"MessageFormat placeholders are zero-based argument indexes, so {1} is Bob and {0} is Ann. Ann owes Bob is tempting if you assume arguments fill placeholders left to right.","Domain 10: MessageFormat",["Placeholders refer to arguments by index, so {1} is the second argument, Bob.","{1} has a matching argument, so it is replaced.",null,"{0} has a matching argument, Ann, so it is replaced."],2],["x60",0,10,"What is the output?\nSystem.out.println(NumberFormat.getInstance(Locale.GERMANY).format(1234.5));",["1.234,5","1,234.5","1 234,5","1234,5"],0,"The German locale uses a period for grouping and a comma as the decimal separator, giving 1.234,5. 1 234,5 is tempting because some European locales, such as French, group with a space.","Domain 10: Formatting numbers with NumberFormat",[null,"That is the US format; the German locale swaps the grouping and decimal separators.","German formatting uses a period, not a space, for grouping.","The default number format uses grouping, so a thousands separator appears."],2]]);
